     "whyNoContribution": "Those seven surfaces bind at runtime through the host's own service seams (ctx.get(id, false) then service.register(...)); at v0.15 the manifest mediates only contributes.commands, contributes.panels (must be empty) and vendor x- points whose registered kinds are tui.dsh/v1alpha1#SettingsSection and #Scene. This plugin registers no host Command through the Command capability, and it does not pass its settings section or scene through the manifest projection, so contributes.commands is declared empty instead of asserting a contribution the code does not make.",
-    "decisionEvents": "tui.dsh/v1alpha1#DecisionEvents is declared as an OPTIONAL requirement and keeps the written TUI-visible fallback above; the seam is built ready-but-not-activated, never faked. MEASURED admission chain (dsh-tui 0.10.1 + the vendored registry + a host with no <DATA_DIR>/extension-grants.json): all four session.*.intercept permissions declared above are registry `default: deny`, and a Community v0.15 permission object has no `optional` flag, so negotiate() returns `waiting_authorization` (PERMISSION_NOT_GRANTED); admitInternal accepts ONLY `compatible`/`compatible_degraded` and throws otherwise (src/dsh-adapter/plugin-host.js:391-395), so NO component identity is bound and the host's effect ledger keeps attributing this bundle as `undeclared`. A grant row is therefore required before ADMISSION SUCCEEDS AT ALL — not merely before a decision subscription activates. Concrete unblock: under the host's own `~/.dsh-tui/extension-grants.json` (DATA_DIR), a \"grants\" row keyed `com.mpd-dsh.mpd-tui` granting the four permissions at scopes tui/input, tui/rewind-prompt, tui/session-switch and tui/compact — exactly the hint the host's own /plugins check prints. The other unblock path is a host policy decision to default-allow first-party TUI bundles; the host's public admit() stays unreachable for a profile-installed plugin either way (admit() throws, admitInternal is token-gated, getHostAdmission* has no production caller). ALSO MEASURED, and the reason this paragraph exists: omitting the four permissions altogether negotiates `compatible` — they are declared because the user approved the complete-manifest shape (decision D9), and this text is the honest price of that choice. The seven surfaces listed in runtimeBoundSurfaces need NO permission and NO identity and are the working surface; nothing here may be read as a claim that the admission decision itself is one of them.",
+    "decisionEvents": "tui.dsh/v1alpha1#DecisionEvents is declared as an OPTIONAL requirement and keeps the written TUI-visible fallback above; the seam is built ready-but-not-activated, never faked. MEASURED admission chain (dsh-tui 0.10.1 + the vendored registry + a host with no <DATA_DIR>/extension-grants.json): all four session.*.intercept permissions declared above are registry `default: deny`, and a Community v0.15 permission object has no `optional` flag, so negotiate() returns `waiting_authorization` (PERMISSION_NOT_GRANTED); admitInternal accepts ONLY `compatible`/`compatible_degraded` and throws otherwise (src/dsh-adapter/plugin-host.js:391-395), so NO component identity is bound and the host's effect ledger keeps attributing this bundle as `undeclared`. EXPECTED, NOT ANOMALOUS: with those four permissions declared and denied by default, `waiting_authorization` is what the host reports and our rows stay `undeclared` in the effect ledger UNTIL a grant row exists — no identity, no decision subscription, and no attributed effect is claimed before that. Declaring the four is deliberate rather than a completeness flourish: the host's decision guard resolves an intercept permission per event at subscribe time (src/dsh-adapter/decision-guard.js) and admission cross-checks the declaration against the DecisionEvents requirement, so a manifest that omitted them could never activate the seam even after grants — they keep it ready-but-not-activated instead of ready-but-permanently-impossible. A grant row is therefore required before ADMISSION SUCCEEDS AT ALL — not merely before a decision subscription activates. Concrete unblock: under the host's own `~/.dsh-tui/extension-grants.json` (DATA_DIR), a \"grants\" row keyed `com.mpd-dsh.mpd-tui` granting the four permissions at scopes tui/input, tui/rewind-prompt, tui/session-switch and tui/compact — exactly the hint the host's own /plugins check prints. The other unblock path is a host policy decision to default-allow first-party TUI bundles; the host's public admit() stays unreachable for a profile-installed plugin either way (admit() throws, admitInternal is token-gated, getHostAdmission* has no production caller). ALSO MEASURED, and the reason this paragraph exists: omitting the four permissions altogether negotiates `compatible` — they are declared because the user approved the complete-manifest shape (decision D9), and this text is the honest price of that choice. The seven surfaces listed in runtimeBoundSurfaces need NO permission and NO identity and are the working surface; nothing here may be read as a claim that the admission decision itself is one of them.",
     "identityDeviation": "HONEST DEVIATION, not an oversight (user decisions D4/D5/D6): the dsh-TUI ecosystem convention for third-party plugins is the @dsh-tui-ecosystem/<name> package scope, an MIT licence, and one manifest per package. This bundle deliberately follows NONE of those three: the identity is @mpd-dsh/mpd-tui, the licence stays SUL-1.0 (this repository's own LICENSE.md, inherited from upstream), and there is exactly ONE bundle-level dsh-plugin.json at the repository root covering the whole @mpd-dsh/mpd bundle rather than one manifest per package. Recorded in the manifest so no reader infers the convention from silence; the bilingual TUI docs (docs/tui.md / docs/tui.zh-CN.md) carry the same deviation.",
